<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kutai Kartanegara Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1960 CE (approx.) · capital Kutai Lama (later Tenggarong)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Malay-Dayak sultanate on the Mahakam River in East Kalimantan, which absorbed ancient Kutai Martadipura and ruled until 1960.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to the Salasilah <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kutai">Kutai</a> chronicle, the kingdom was founded around 1300 at Kutai Lama near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahakam-river">Mahakam River</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aji-batara-agung-dewa-sakti">Aji Batara Agung Dewa Sakti</a>. It was at first a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> state that looked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>, while upriver lay the old Kingdom of Kutai, known as Martadipura.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was adopted around 1575. In 1635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aji-pangeran-sinum-panji-mendapa">Aji Pangeran Sinum Panji Mendapa</a> conquered Martadipura and joined the two names. The sultans traded rattan, gold and birds' nests from the interior with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> merchants, and in 1782 moved their capital upriver to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenggarong">Tenggarong</a>. In the colonial era <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and oil brought new wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After a clash with British adventurer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-erskine-murray">James Erskine Murray</a>, the sultan accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> overlordship in 1844. The sultanate became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and was abolished in 1960 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Aji Muhammad Parikesit. It was revived as a cultural institution in 2001, and its palace is now a museum.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kutai-kartanegara-sultanate.jpg" alt="Kutai Kartanegara Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ethelium · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Sultanate_of_Kutai.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1960 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kutai Lama (later Tenggarong)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aji Batara Agung Dewa Sakti, Aji Pangeran Sinum Panji Mendapa, Aji Muhammad Parikesit</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kutai Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kutai Kartanegara, Kutai Kartanegara ing Martadipura</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1630&amp;focus=kutai-kartanegara-sultanate" aria-label="Kutai Kartanegara Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7939 2199 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kutai Kartanegara Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8259 2455L8251 2424M8259 2455L7959 2663M8259 2455L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8251 2424h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8259 2455h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8259 2455h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kutai Lama. <a href="/atlas?year=1630&amp;focus=kutai-kartanegara-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
